# SYNOPSIS

```usage
chroot dir [cmd [arg ...]]
```

# DESCRIPTION

`chroot` makes <dir> the root directory, changes to it, and runs <cmd> with its <arg>s, found by `PATH`.
With no <cmd> it runs `$SHELL -i`, or `/bin/sh -i` if `SHELL` is not set.

On VectraOS it always fails: the C library does not implement `chroot`, so the call fails with `Function not implemented` and nothing is run.
A namespace (see namespace(6) and ns(1)) is how a VectraOS program is given another root.

`chroot` is sbase's, a hard link to `/boot/bin/posix/sbase-box`; there is no native one.
In the POSIX namespace (a manifest's `ns=posix`, made by `/lib/ns/posix`) `/boot/bin/posix` is bound before `/boot/bin` on `/bin`, so a POSIX program finds it as `/bin/chroot`.

# SEE ALSO

namespace(6), ns(1); sbase's page is `third_party/sbase/chroot.1` in the repository

# DIAGNOSTICS

With no <dir> or with any flag it prints `usage: chroot dir [cmd [arg ...]]` and exits 1.
A failed `chroot` or `chdir` is reported, as `chroot: chroot /n/x: Function not implemented`, and it exits 1.
If <cmd> cannot be run it says so and exits 127 when it was not found, 126 otherwise.

# BUGS

It cannot work on VectraOS, as above; the C library also writes `vx-musl: system call ... is not implemented` to the debug console the first time.
There is no `/bin/sh`, so even where `chroot` worked, running it with no <cmd> would fail.
